Q: Draw an outline outside the path surface A: <p>I have recently faced this issue and found out a solution, which requires no additional canvas or usage of destination-in destination-out compositing mode.  It uses the default compositing mode and require some maths and trigonometry.</p>
<p>I have explained the steps to calculate the outline points, with visuals (unfortunately I have just created an account for this answer, the images are being displayed but in the link provided) and included the code.</p>
<p>There is a snippet of a simple demo, which you can modify the points in the code to make your own desire shape to check their outline.</p>
<h2>Drawing Shape to canvas</h2>
<ul>
<li><p>I have an array of <code>points</code> defining the shape. A point has a x component, <code>point.x</code> and y component, <code>point.y</code>.</p>
</li>
<li><p><code>CanvasRenderingContext2D.beginPath()</code></p>
</li>
<li><p><code>CanvasRenderingContext2D.moveTo(points[0].x, points[0].y)</code></p>
</li>
<li><p>Looping with each point <code>CanvasRenderingContext2D.lineTo(points[i].x, points[i].y)</code></p>
</li>
<li><p>Set the colour <code>CanvasRendingContext2D.fillStyle = colour</code></p>
</li>
<li><p>Finally using <code>CanvasRenderingContext2D.fill()</code> to draw a line from first point and last point</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p>Here's the code:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>/**
 * Draw a shape by making straight lines between points.
 * 
 * @param {CanvasRenderingContext2D} The context 2d instance
 * @param {Vector2[]} points The points in world space.
 * @param {String} colour The colour of the shape.
 * 
 * @returns {boolean} False if did not provide enough point to create a shape.
 */
function DrawShapeUsingPoints(ctx, points, colour){
    //not a closed area
    if(points.length &lt; 3){
        return false;
    }

    //start drawing
    ctx.beginPath();

    //move to start of line
    ctx.moveTo(points[0].x, points[0].y);

    //loop to draw points
    for (let i = 1; i &lt; points.length; i++){
        //draw line
        ctx.lineTo(points[i].x, points[i].y);
    }

    ctx.fillStyle = colour;
    ctx.fill();

    return true;
}
</code></pre>
<h2>Steps to calculate the outline points</h2>
<ul>
<li><p>In this image you can see a red point, a blue line (connecting previous point to the red point), a purple line (connecting red point to the next point) and direction of the line in black. <a href="https://i.sstatic.net/6kZUCbBM.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Showing point, line connecting to point and direction of line</a></p>
</li>
<li><p>Then get the <code>angle</code> between the two direction, define the width of the outline as <code>x</code> and find the length of the hypotenuse <code>h</code>, which is <code>h = x / Math.sin(Math.PI - angle)</code>. <a href="https://i.sstatic.net/Dz5Uk24E.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Finding hypotenuse, h</a></p>
</li>
<li><p>Then we find the distance between the red point and where the outline should be, by using <code>d = 2 * (h * Math.cos(angle * 0.5))</code>. <a href="https://i.sstatic.net/QwSyI1nZ.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Finding the distance between red point and its outline</a></p>
<ul>
<li><p>The above steps were for angle &gt;= π/2</p>
</li>
<li><p>For angle = π, <code>d = x</code></p>
</li>
<li><p>For angle &lt;= π/2, use the following <code>d = 2 * x * Math.cos(angle * 0.5) / Math.cos((Math.PI * 0.5) - angle)</code></p>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><p>Now since we have the distance between point, we just need to calculate by:</p>
<ul>
<li><p>adding the direction of the two lines together</p>
</li>
<li><p>normalise the resulting direction</p>
</li>
<li><p>multiple the normalised direction with <code>d</code> and add it to the position of the red point</p>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><p>Finally now since we have the point of where the outline should be, we just check if it's inside the shape and if it is, we recalculate the point and instead of using <code>d</code>, we use <code>-d</code>.</p>
<ul>
<li><p>For the first point, you need to use ray casting algorithm, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Point_in_polygon" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Point_in_polygon</a>. If it's inside use <code>-d</code> instead of <code>d</code>.</p>
</li>
<li><p>For the rest of the points:</p>
<ul>
<li><p>generate two points using <code>d</code> and <code>-d</code></p>
</li>
<li><p>for each of the two new points, get the direction from the last calculated point to it and normalise it</p>
</li>
<li><p>for each of the directions, get the dot product of this direction and direction of the black line (from previous point to red point)</p>
</li>
<li><p>The dot product closer to 1, is the correct point to use for the outline</p>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
<h2>Adjustment for the algorithm</h2>
<p>Here is the outline generated, in my personal project, using the steps above: <a href="https://i.sstatic.net/mLUCcm1D.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer">outline of non-regular shape</a>.</p>
<ul>
<li><p>However when the object is scaled down, creating small angle between the two direction of the line, the outline extend to large scale.</p>
<ul>
<li><p>Here's the canvas: <a href="https://i.sstatic.net/XIaJZ88c.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer">outline of non-regular shape, extending to large scale</a>.</p>
</li>
<li><p>To fix that, I clamp the <code>angle</code> to a minimum of 20 degree. Here's the fix: <a href="https://i.sstatic.net/6oP7e6BM.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer">outline of non-regular shape, with angled clamped to a minimum of 20 degree</a></p>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><p>Part of outline is jittery when checking if <code>angle</code> is exactly π and using <code>d = x</code>, but we clamp the <code>angle</code> to a minimum of 20 degree, there's no need to check for <code>angle == x</code></p>
</li>
</ul>
<h2>Code for generating outline points</h2>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>/**
 * @param {Vector2[]} points The points defining the shape.
 * @param {Number} outlineWidth The distance between outline edge and object edge.
 */
function GetShapeOutlinePoints(points, outlineWidth){
    //hold the new points
    const offsettedPoint = new Array(points.length);

    //loop through each points
    for (let i = 0; i &lt; points.length; i++) {
        //get direction from this point to next point
        let currentLineDir = new Vector2(
            points[i].x - points[(i+1) % points.length].x,
            points[i].y- points[(i+1) % points.length].y
        ).Normalised;
        //get direction from previous point to this point
        let previousLineDir = i == 0 ? 
            new Vector2(
                points[i].x - points[points.length-1].x,
                points[i].y- points[points.length-1].y
            ).Normalised : 
            new Vector2(
                points[i].x - points[i-1].x,
                points[i].y- points[i-1].y
            ).Normalised; 

        //get angle from the two directions
        const angle = Vector2.GetAngle(currentLineDir, previousLineDir);

        //the two directions added together and normalised
        const desireDir = new Vector2(currentLineDir.x + previousLineDir.x, currentLineDir.y + previousLineDir.y).Normalised;


        //hold the actual distance for calculating point
        let d;
        //halfPI, equivalent 90degree
        const halfPI = Math.PI * 0.5;

        //the minimum angle allowed, otherwise outline at edges where angle &lt; 20
        //will blow up to large scale
        const minAngle = 20 * (Math.PI / 180);
        const c_angle = Math.max(minAngle, angle);

        /**
         * Straight line, didnt use it, since we clamp at 20 degree
         * Even if we didn't use the clamping, checking for this make
         * the outline jittery, so we just issue it
         *
        if(angle == Math.PI){
            d = outlineWidth;
        }*/

        //for angle greater than or equal to 90
        if(angle &gt;= halfPI){
            const h = outlineWidth / Math.sin(Math.PI - c_angle);
            d = 2 * (h * Math.cos(c_angle * 0.5));

        //for angle less than 90
        } else if(angle &lt; halfPI){
            d = 2 * outlineWidth * Math.cos(c_angle * 0.5) / Math.cos(halfPI - c_angle);
        }

        if(i == 0){
            //Add the raycasting algorithm to check if inside

            //if didn't add and the first point is inside the shape
            //then the whole outline will be incorrect
        } else {
            //calculate the two points
            const point1 = new Vector2(
                points[i].x + desireDir.x * d,
                points[i].y + desireDir.y * d
            );
            const point2 = new Vector2(
                points[i].x + desireDir.x * -d,
                points[i].y + desireDir.y * -d
            );

            //calculate the direction and normalise it
            const newDir1 = new Vector2(
                point1.x - offsettedPoint[i-1].x,
                point1.y- offsettedPoint[i-1].y
            ).Normalised;
            const newDir2 = new Vector2(
                point2.x - offsettedPoint[i-1].x,
                point2.y- offsettedPoint[i-1].y
            ).Normalised;

            //get dot product of both
            let dot1 = Vector2.Dot(previousLineDir, newDir1);
            let dot2 = Vector2.Dot(previousLineDir, newDir2);

            //choose the one closest to 1
            //if dot2 is closest therefore invert sign of d
            if(dot2 &gt; dot1){
                d *= -1;
            }
        }

        //offset and add to array
        offsettedPoint[i] = new Vector2(
            points[i].x + desireDir.x * d,
            points[i].y + desireDir.y * d
        );
    }

    return offsettedPoint;
}
</code></pre>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>class Vector2{
    x

    y;

    get Magnitude(){
        const a = this;
        const b = new Vector2(0 ,0);
        return Math.sqrt((a.x - b.x) * (a.x - b.x) + (a.y - b.y) * (a.y - b.y));
    }

    get Normalised(){
        const magnitude = this.Magnitude;
        return magnitude == 0 ? this : new Vector2(this.x / magnitude, this.y / magnitude);
    }

    constructor(x, y){
        this.x = x;
        this.y = y;
    }

    static Dot(a, b){
        return a.x * b.x + a.y * b.y;
    }

    static GetAngle(a, b){
        return Math.acos(Vector2.Dot(a,b) / (a.Magnitude * b.Magnitude));
    }
}
</code></pre>
<h2>Drawing Shape Outline to canvas</h2>
<p>We draw a new shape using the shape outline points, as well as the shape points and use <code>CanvasRenderingContext2d.fill('evenodd')</code> to fill only the outline.</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>function DrawShapeOutlineUsingPoints(ctx, points, outlinePoints, colour){
    //not a closed area
    if(points.length &lt; 3 || outlinePoints.length &lt; 3){
        return false;
    }

    //start drawing
    ctx.beginPath();

    //move to start of line
    ctx.moveTo(points[0].x, points[0].y);

    //loop to lines of object
    for (let i = 1; i &lt; points.length; i++){
        //create line
        ctx.lineTo(points[i].x, points[i].y);
    }

    //move to start of line
    ctx.moveTo(outlinePoints[0].x, outlinePoints[0].y);

    //loop to draw outline of object
    for (let i = 1; i &lt; points.length; i++){
        //create line
        ctx.lineTo(outlinePoints[i].x, outlinePoints[i].y);
    }

    ctx.fillStyle = colour;
    //use evenodd to fill only the outline
    ctx.fill('evenodd');


    return true;
}
</code></pre>
